const posts = [
  {
    tag: 'E-commerce',
    title: '5 Reasons Your Amazon Listings Aren\'t Converting (And How to Fix Them)',
    body: `
      <p>You've done the hard work of getting traffic to your Amazon listings. But if your conversion rate is sitting below 10–12%, something on the page itself is killing your sales. Here are the five most common culprits I see when auditing client listings.</p>
      <h3>1. Your Main Image Doesn't Stop the Scroll</h3>
      <p>On Amazon, your main image is your billboard. Most sellers use a plain product shot on white and call it a day. But your competitors are investing in lifestyle imagery, infographics, and high-contrast visuals that stand out in search results. If your image looks like everyone else's, shoppers scroll past.</p>
      <h3>2. Your Title Is Stuffed With Keywords But Says Nothing</h3>
      <p>A keyword-heavy title might help you rank, but it doesn't help you convert. Buyers scan titles for relevance and clarity. Lead with your most important benefit or use case, then layer in keywords naturally.</p>
      <h3>3. Your Bullet Points List Features, Not Benefits</h3>
      <p>Features tell. Benefits sell. "Made with 100% cotton" is a feature. "Stays soft after 50 washes — no shrinking, no pilling" is a benefit. Rewrite every bullet from the buyer's perspective: what problem does this solve?</p>
      <h3>4. You Have No A+ Content</h3>
      <p>If you're brand registered and not using A+ content, you're leaving conversion rate on the table. A+ content lets you add comparison charts, lifestyle images, and brand story modules that build trust and reduce buyer hesitation.</p>
      <h3>5. Your Reviews Are Lagging Behind Competitors</h3>
      <p>Social proof is the silent closer. If your competitors have 500 reviews and you have 40, buyers will choose them even if your product is better. Enrol in Amazon's Vine program for new products and use the Request a Review button consistently.</p>
      <p><strong>The fix:</strong> Run a full listing audit against your top three competitors. For every element — image, title, bullets, A+, reviews — ask honestly: are we winning or losing this comparison?</p>
    `
  },
  {
    tag: 'Google Ads',
    title: 'Why Your Google Ads ROAS Is Low — And the Fix Most Agencies Miss',
    body: `
      <p>A low ROAS on Google Ads is one of the most common complaints I hear from businesses who've tried working with agencies. The agency blames the budget. The client blames the agency. The real problem is usually neither.</p>
      <h3>The First Thing to Check: Search Term Reports</h3>
      <p>Most agencies set up campaigns and let them run. What they don't do consistently is dig into the search term report — the actual queries that triggered your ads. If you're running broad or phrase match keywords without aggressive negative keyword lists, you're almost certainly paying for irrelevant traffic that will never convert.</p>
      <h3>Your Landing Page Is the Real Problem</h3>
      <p>This is the fix most agencies miss because it's outside their scope. They optimize the ad. They ignore the page it sends traffic to. If your landing page is slow, confusing, or doesn't match the ad's promise, your Quality Score drops, your CPCs rise, and your conversion rate tanks. The ad and the landing page are one system.</p>
      <h3>You're Bidding on the Wrong Campaigns</h3>
      <p>Performance Max campaigns are powerful but opaque. If you're running PMax without proper asset groups, audience signals, and brand exclusions, Google will spend your budget on whatever it decides is relevant — which often isn't what you'd choose.</p>
      <h3>Attribution Is Lying to You</h3>
      <p>Last-click attribution makes Google Ads look worse than it is for upper-funnel campaigns and better than it is for branded terms. Switch to data-driven attribution and look at assisted conversions before making budget decisions.</p>
      <p><strong>The fix:</strong> Pull your search term report right now. Add negative keywords for anything that isn't directly relevant. Then load your landing page on mobile and time how long it takes to load. Fix those two things before touching your bids.</p>
